[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JavaScript Control Flow: if/else, switch, and Loops"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Control flow statements are fundamental to programming. They allow you to execute code conditionally and repeatedly. In JavaScript, the primary control flow mechanisms are 'if/else' statements, 'switch' statements, and various loop constructs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"if/else Statements"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'if/else' statement allows you to execute different blocks of code based on a condition. The 'if' statement evaluates a boolean expression. If the expression is 'true', the code block within the 'if' statement is executed. Otherwise, the code block within the optional 'else' statement is executed."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"let age = 20;

if (age >= 18) {
  console.log('You are an adult.');
} else {
  console.log('You are a minor.');
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also chain 'if/else' statements using 'else if' to handle multiple conditions."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"let score = 75;

if (score >= 90) {
  console.log('A');
} else if (score >= 80) {
  console.log('B');
} else if (score >= 70) {
  console.log('C');
} else {
  console.log('D');
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"switch Statement"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'switch' statement provides a more concise way to handle multiple conditions based on the value of a single expression. Each 'case' within the 'switch' statement represents a different possible value. The 'break' statement is crucial to prevent fall-through to the next 'case'."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"let day = 'Wednesday';

switch (day) {
  case 'Monday':
    console.log('Start of the week');
    break;
  case 'Tuesday':
  case 'Wednesday':
    console.log('Mid-week');
    break;
  case 'Thursday':
    console.log('Almost there!');
    break;
  case 'Friday':
    console.log('Weekend is coming!');
    break;
  default:
    console.log('Weekend!');
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'default' case is executed if none of the other 'case' values match the expression."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Loops"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Loops allow you to repeatedly execute a block of code. JavaScript provides several loop constructs:"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'for' loop: Executes a block of code a specified number of times."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'while' loop: Executes a block of code as long as a condition is 'true'."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'do...while' loop: Similar to 'while', but executes the block of code at least once."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'for...in' loop: Iterates over the properties of an object."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'for...of' loop: Iterates over the values of an iterable object (e.g., arrays, strings, Maps, Sets)."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"for Loop"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'for' loop consists of three parts: initialization (let i = 0), condition (i < 5), and increment (i++). The loop continues as long as the condition is 'true'."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"while Loop"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"let i = 0;

while (i < 5) {
  console.log(i);
  i++;
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'while' loop continues executing as long as the condition (i < 5) is 'true'. It's crucial to ensure that the condition eventually becomes 'false' to avoid an infinite loop."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"do...while Loop"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"let i = 0;

do {
  console.log(i);
  i++;
} while (i < 5);"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'do...while' loop is similar to the 'while' loop, but it guarantees that the code block is executed at least once, even if the condition is initially 'false'."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"for...in Loop"},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"const person = {
  name: 'John',
  age: 30,
  city: 'New York'
};

for (let key in person) {
  console.log(key + ': ' + person[key]);
}"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'for...in' loop iterates over the property names (keys) of an object.  It's generally recommended to use 'Object.keys(object).forEach()' or 'for...of' with 'Object.entries(object)' for better control and predictability."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"for...of Loop"},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

for (let number of numbers) {
  console.log(number);
}"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'for...of' loop iterates over the values of an iterable object, such as an array. It provides a cleaner and more direct way to access the elements of the iterable."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"break and continue Statements"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'break' statement is used to exit a loop prematurely. The 'continue' statement skips the current iteration and proceeds to the next one."},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  if (i === 3) {
    continue; // Skip iteration when i is 3
  }
  if (i === 7) {
    break; // Exit the loop when i is 7
  }
  console.log(i);
}"}]
